#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186331290 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "624215ff-efdc-5b12-8d61-9d66371672d9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Valid Region to Define a Double Integral Over a Non-Rectangular Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27b4ea34-20c6-59f6-9f96-7af7286e254c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331292} {:answer/id #uuid "d70435ac-0cb1-51ff-9927-23e5a373192d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331293} {:answer/id #uuid "e5fa6afe-d15d-51e8-b0d3-cbec03bd754b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331294} {:answer/id #uuid "ba6600cd-cc42-52cb-ae1d-aa65e55a2958" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331295} {:answer/id #uuid "cff86dae-f973-5703-8666-822171e35fc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331296}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cff86dae-f973-5703-8666-822171e35fc3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331296} :answer-field/id #uuid "06a3b41d-ccd8-5656-b35c-dd74bbb0706c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331291}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "41942a74-5957-5b5b-8aba-bd38af9f8d4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-80503" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14053940/assets/9d4a0a08513d817fe841f1e45e5d363d6e64241837dea71f38aef452f86b08a7.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{x}, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{∬}_{D}\\frac{3{y}^{2}}{x}\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nRequire $D\\subseteq R$, so the rectangle contains the entire region. Which of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\n- $R=[0,1]\\times [-1,1]$\n- $R=[0,2]\\times [-1,1]$\n- $R=[0,2]\\times [0,1]$" :question/worked-solution "The displayed region includes points with $x>1$, so rectangle I, $[0,1]\\times[-1,1]$, does not contain all of $D$. It includes points with $y<0$, so rectangle III, $[0,2]\\times[0,1]$, also fails the containment requirement. The entire drawn region lies in rectangle II, $[0,2]\\times[-1,1]$.\n\nThe region lies strictly to the right of the $y$-axis, so $3y^2/x$ is defined throughout $D$. For rectangle II, $f$ equals this integrand on $D$ and zero outside $D$ in $R$; in particular its zero branch applies on $x=0$, which is outside $D$. Hence $\\iint_R f\\,dA=\\iint_D3y^2/x\\,dA$. The correct answer is \"II only\"."}]}
